# Current theses

## Thesis T-migrated-conformance: Preserve reviewed project guidance
Status: accepted
Based-on: user-reviewed-contract-migration

### Guidance
<folder_context path="conformance">

<application_context name="git-a2a schema 2 implementation conformance corpus">
This subtree is a language-neutral executable contract for schema 2. It tests only the five public
domain commands through a supplied binary and never imports the reference implementation. A second
implementation clones the repository, sets CONFORMANCE_BIN, and receives the same cases and verdicts.
</application_context>

<settled_decision name="Version 2 cases are sequential filesystem contracts">
Every numbered directory under cases/ contains manifest/ as the initial working directory, command
as a JSON array of argv arrays, and expected/exit-code. Invocations run in order in the same fresh
temporary repository and stop at the first non-zero exit. Optional expected/stdout and stderr contain
one Go-compatible regular expression per non-comment line; ! negates a pattern. Files under
expected/files/ match byte-for-byte, expected/absent paths must not exist, and
expected/file-patterns.json checks required syntax. env.json adds environment values.
</settled_decision>

<system_invariant name="The runner treats the implementation as an external program">
CONFORMANCE_BIN names one executable. The runner invokes it without a shell, observes only public
stdout, stderr, exit status, and filesystem effects, and never calls an internal package. Cases use
only acme-* identities and have no credentials or public network dependency.
</system_invariant>

<settled_decision name="Git lifecycle fixtures are local and revision-consistent">
git-fixture.json creates one tracked source commit and a local bare remote, optionally initializing
the consumer as a Git repository. {{GIT_URL}} and {{GIT_COMMIT}} are the only source tokens. The
five-command lifecycle case proves init, add, list, pull, and remove in one repository, with the same
commit used for the submodule, lock metadata, agent discovery, and surface materialization.
</settled_decision>

<settled_decision name="Conformance versions independently from CLI releases">
conformance/VERSION is the integer protocol and contract revision. Version 2 intentionally breaks
the schema 1 runner protocol: command is now a sequence, and removed HTTP/contact, disposable-cache,
shell-fixture, and platform-skip facilities are not accepted.
</settled_decision>

<scope_boundary name="Schema 2 public behavior">
Cover init, add, pull, remove, local list text/JSON, schema 1 and dual-manifest rejection, and
representative removed-command rejection. Adapter-specific corpora and unit tests own the full
platform matrix; conformance uses the real submodule fallback to exercise lifecycle without external
package managers.
</scope_boundary>

<compilation_and_testing_pipeline name="Conformance gates">
CONFORMANCE_BIN=&lt;absolute binary&gt; go run ./conformance/runner must pass on Linux and Windows.
go test ./conformance/runner covers protocol parsing and normalization. The corpus must exercise all
five domain commands and must not require network access beyond its local bare Git fixture.
</compilation_and_testing_pipeline>

</folder_context>

### Rationale
Migrated from the pre-2.0 HINT source format without changing its guidance.

